        "created_at": 1794507269.8905742
      },
      "rev_n_1_2f2900_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_1_2f2900_0",
        "node_id": "n_1_2f2900",
        "thought_text": "Analyze the problem as a linear Diophantine equation: find non-negative integers k_i such that sum(k_i * A) ≡ C (mod B). This simplifies to checking if C is a multiple of gcd(A, B).",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794507273.4942882
      },
      "rev_n_2_db2231_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_2_db2231_0",
        "node_id": "n_2_db2231",
        "thought_text": "Compute g = gcd(A, B). The set of all possible sums of multiples of A modulo B forms the subgroup generated by g in Z_B. The target C is reachable if and only if C % g == 0.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794507273.4943135
      },
      "rev_n_3_a15b3d_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_3_a15b3d_0",
        "node_id": "n_3_a15b3d",
        "thought_text": "Implement a direct check: read A, B, C from stdin, calculate the greatest common divisor of A and B, and print YES if C is divisible by that GCD, otherwise NO.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794507273.4943273
      },
      "rev_n_4_165778_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_4_165778_0",
        "node_id": "n_4_165778",
        "thought_text": "Calculate the greatest common divisor (GCD) of A and B. The set of all possible linear combinations of A modulo B forms the subgroup generated by gcd(A, B). Therefore, a target remainder C is achievable if and only if C is divisible by gcd(A, B).",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-6227689040590010780",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794507288.3368971
      },
      "rev_n_5_51a1a3_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_5_51a1a3_0",
        "node_id": "n_5_51a1a3",
        "thought_text": "Since we can select any number of multiples of A, the problem reduces to checking if C lies in the ideal generated by A within the ring of integers modulo B. This is mathematically equivalent to verifying if C % gcd(A, B) == 0.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-6227689040590010780",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794507288.3369195
      },
      "rev_n_6_1e3548_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_6_1e3548_0",
        "node_id": "n_6_1e3548",
        "thought_text": "Verify the edge case where C is 0. Since we must select at least one integer, we can simply choose A itself (or any multiple) if A % B == 0, or combine multiples to reach 0 mod B if gcd(A, B) divides 0 (which is always true). The GCD condition holds universally for C=0 as well.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-6227689040590010780",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794507288.3369334
      },
      "rev_n_7_cd76c7_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_7_cd76c7_0",
        "node_id": "n_7_cd76c7",
        "thought_text": "Check if C is divisible by the greatest common divisor of A and B, since the set of reachable sums modulo B forms the subgroup generated by gcd(A, B).",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-854704084965669107",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794507294.435051
      },
      "rev_n_8_4330e2_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_8_4330e2_0",
        "node_id": "n_8_4330e2",
        "thought_text": "Iterate through multiples of A (A, 2A, 3A, ...) modulo B until either C is found or the cycle repeats without hitting C, leveraging the fact that the sequence of remainders is periodic with period at most B.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-854704084965669107",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794507294.4350674
      },
      "rev_n_9_8263d6_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_9_8263d6_0",
        "node_id": "n_9_8263d6",
        "thought_text": "Construct a small brute-force search using dynamic programming or BFS on the state space of remainders modulo B to verify reachability, ensuring correctness for small constraints.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-854704084965669107",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794507294.4350753
      },
      "rev_n_10_e561e8_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_10_e561e8_0",
        "node_id": "n_10_e561e8",
        "thought_text": "Analyze the linear combination property: any sum of multiples of A is a multiple of A, so the sum S must satisfy S % A == 0. We need S % B == C. This implies S = k*A for some integer k, so k*A % B == C. This is a linear congruence equation k*A ≡ C (mod B), which has a solution if and only if gcd(A, B) divides C.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-1993453325971426176",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794507303.767607
      },
      "rev_n_11_647647_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_11_647647_0",
        "node_id": "n_11_647647",
        "thought_text": "Use the Frobenius Coin Problem logic (Chicken McNugget Theorem) adapted for modular arithmetic. Since we can pick any number of integers, we are effectively asking if C is in the subgroup generated by A within the additive group Z_B. The subgroup generated by A modulo B consists of all multiples of gcd(A, B) modulo B. Thus, check if C is a multiple of gcd(A, B).",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-1993453325971426176",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794507303.7676272
      },
      "rev_n_12_898bcf_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_12_898bcf_0",
        "node_id": "n_12_898bcf",
        "thought_text": "Simulate a Breadth-First Search (BFS) on the remainders modulo B. Start with remainder 0 (representing sum 0, though we must pick at least one, we can reach any reachable state). From any remainder r, adding A moves to (r + A) % B. If we can reach state C, output YES. Since the state space is finite (size B), this will terminate. This is a constructive verification of the mathematical condition.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-1993453325971426176",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794507303.7676353
      },
      "rev_n_13_1826db_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_13_1826db_0",
        "node_id": "n_13_1826db",
        "thought_text": "Compute g = gcd(A, B) and check if C is divisible by g. If C % g == 0, output YES; otherwise NO.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "1815831348488641419",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794507344.2865624
      },
      "rev_n_14_024b51_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_14_024b51_0",
        "node_id": "n_14_024b51",
        "thought_text": "Iterate through all possible counts of integers (1 to B) and check if any combination of multiples of A sums to C mod B using BFS or dynamic programming.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "1815831348488641419",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794507344.2865796
      },
      "rev_n_15_0762ba_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_15_0762ba_0",
        "node_id": "n_15_0762ba",
        "thought_text": "Since the problem is mathematically equivalent to checking if C lies in the ideal generated by A in Z_B, verify the condition C % gcd(A, B) == 0 directly.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "1815831348488641419",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794507344.2865891
      },
      "rev_n_16_5c0b09_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_16_5c0b09_0",
        "node_id": "n_16_5c0b09",
        "thought_text": "Calculate G = gcd(A, B). The set of all possible sums of multiples of A modulo B is exactly the set of multiples of G modulo B. Therefore, the condition is simply whether C % G == 0.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-8572115642647467674",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794507355.3166084
      },
      "rev_n_17_4f372d_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_17_4f372d_0",
        "node_id": "n_17_4f372d",
        "thought_text": "Since we can select any number of integers, we can effectively generate any multiple of A. The problem reduces to checking if C is in the subgroup generated by A in the additive group Z_B, which is mathematically equivalent to checking divisibility by gcd(A, B).",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-8572115642647467674",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794507355.3166256
      },
      "rev_n_18_105134_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_18_105134_0",
        "node_id": "n_18_105134",
